  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nThe pressure gradient that is resulting in today's gusty winds \nrelaxes tonight as the low pressure center continues eastward \nover Atlantic Canada. Winds will subside, but enough of a breeze\nmay persist to keep valley fog from forming. This breeze along \nwith passing clouds are also likely to be enough to keep most \nlocations from decoupling tonight, but it will still be chilly. \nThose north of the mountains should see temperatures fall into \nthe low to mid-40s, while the foothills and points south range \nfrom the upper 40s to the mid-50s.\n\nUpper level flow turns northwesterly Friday as troughing takes \nhold over the area. There will also be a slight tightening of \nthe pressure gradient again as high pressure begins to build in \nfrom the west, with northwesterly wind gusts only expected to be\naround 20 mph this time around. The cooler airmass is going to \nbe apparent as high temperatures only climb into the low to \nmid-60s north of the mountains, and into the low to mid-70s in \nthe south. High pressure begins to crest overhead Friday night, \nso strong radiational cooling is all but guaranteed to bring \nabout a cold night. I tailored the forecast heavily toward MOS \nguidance which handles these situations better than the NBM \nwhich results in widespread mid to upper 30s north of the \nmountains. A patchy frost is possible, but also isolated enough \nthat I don't feel an advisory is warranted at this time. \nHowever, anyone with sensitive plants or crops outside is going \nto want to take precautions to keep them from harm. Valley fog \nis also likely. South of the mountains temperatures are going to\nfall into the low to mid-40s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure will start to slide off into the Atlantic on \nSaturday as an upper level ridge axis moves overhead. These \nconditions will lead to fair weather and highs mainly in the \n70s.\n\nWe transition back into southwest flow aloft Saturday night as \nthe next trough/front starts to approach and clouds increase. \nWarm air advection and cloud cover may lead to some relatively \nwarmer lows so did blend in a little bit of NBM90 to better \naccount for these trends. Lows will mainly range from the lower \n50s north, to the mid/upper 50s along the Main Coast, and up to \nthe lower 60s across southern New Hampshire. \n\nThe next low to medium rain chances (20 to 50 percent) then \nreturn to the forecast Sunday morning in the form of scattered \nrain showers ahead of the cold front. Sunday's rain continues to\nappear low impact as NBM probabilities for a quarter inch of \nrain or more only max out around 50 percent over southern New \nHampshire and far southwest Maine. Probabilities for a half inch\nor more max out around 40 percent over the same area and only \n10 to 20 percent for an inch or more. Thus, we shouldn't see a \nwash out. The better chance for showers shifts north Sunday \nafternoon and into the evening along the cold front as it moves \ninto the forecast area. Some of the models are suggesting some \nmodest instability so a rumble or two of thunder is not out of \nthe question in the afternoon or early evening. Any lingering \nshowers will likely move out by early Monday.\n\nRidging then moves across the region, leading to mostly dry \nconditions to start the work week but the next front will bring \nmore low impact precipitation chances mid to late week.",
